--TEST--
No `Core\Http\Client` member has a spelling for an unbounded wait: every bound is a `Duration` that cannot be null, and the options bag is a closed set of keys — `rule:http-server/no-spelling-for-an-unbounded-wait`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:http-server/no-spelling-for-an-unbounded-wait`: there is no spelling for "wait forever". `Duration` has no
// infinite value, `deadline` is not nullable, and omitting the option inherits
// `[http.client]`'s own figure rather than removing the bound — so the absence
// of a spelling is the guarantee, and no check anywhere has to hold it up.
//
// The depth claim is *agreement* again: the same refusal asked of all five
// members, so a member that grew a nullable bound of its own fails here rather
// than at a review.
string $url = "http://example.com/status";

var $a = Core\Http\Client::get($url, {deadline: null});
var $b = Core\Http\Client::post($url, {deadline: null});
var $c = Core\Http\Client::put($url, {connectTimeout: null});
var $d = Core\Http\Client::delete($url, {retryBackoff: null});
var $e = Core\Http\Client::head($url, {deadline: null});

// The other half of "no spelling": the bag's keys are a closed set, so a
// caller cannot reach an unbounded wait by naming an option that does not
// exist and having it ignored. The help line is the whole roster, which is
// what makes an option quietly added or removed visible here.
var $f = Core\Http\Client::get($url, {timeout: 30s});
--EXPECT--

